## Introduction

In the [previous post](./rethinking-observability-with-clickhouse), we explored why ClickHouse is a powerful choice for observability. Now it's time to get hands-on. This post guides you through setting up ClickHouse from scratch using Docker, with a focus on preparing it for high-throughput log ingestion.

Whether you're experimenting locally or laying the foundation for a multi-node deployment, this setup provides a reliable starting point.

---

## Deployment Options

ClickHouse supports several [deployment options](https://clickhouse.com/docs/install) depending on your environment and goals:

| Method               | Best Use Case                                                                                                                          |
| -------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **Binary (.tar.gz)** | Bare metal servers with full control                                                                                                   |
| **Docker**           | Quick local setup and dev testing                                                                                                      |
| **Kubernetes**       | Scalable production environments from [Altinity Kubernetes Operator](https://altinity.com/kubernetes-operator/)                        |
| **Cloud Services**   | Fully managed setup via [ClickHouse Cloud](https://clickhouse.com/cloud) or [Altinity.Cloud](https://altinity.com/managed-clickhouse/) |

For this guide, we’ll focus on Docker, which is fast to set up and ideal for development and prototyping.

---

## Running ClickHouse via Docker Compose

ClickHouse has an example repo with various Docker Compose Recipes. For this article, let's use [this simple 1 ClickHouse node and 1 ClickHouse Keeper recipe](https://github.com/ClickHouse/examples/tree/main/docker-compose-recipes/recipes/ch-1S_1K). Follow the instructions from the repository to setup.

Assuming you have a docker setup, once you cloned the repository, just run:

```bash
docker compose up -d
```
